// Compare up to three condominiums, side by side, on what the public record can actually say.
//
// ROWS is emitted mechanically from data/live.json in the sg-property-decision repo (URA
// PMI_Resi_Transaction resale medians per project, twelve months to August 2026; nearest MRT
// from LTA station exits; district size-band medians from the same feed). It is generated by
// scripts, not hand-typed, and the count, the district coverage and each project's standing
// against its district are all recomputed from ROWS so the prose cannot drift from the data.
//
// INCLUSION RULE, applied mechanically: a project appears only with at least 10 resales in the
// twelve months. Below that a median moves on one unusual unit, and a comparison tool offering a
// number resting on two sales is worse than one that declines to. 309 of about 1,135 condominiums
// clear it. That is a real bound on coverage, and the figure says so rather than implying the
// list is every condominium in Singapore.
//
// COLOUR MEANS SEGMENT AND NOTHING ELSE (VOICE.md C10), reusing the validated triple from
// DistrictYieldChart and DistrictSupplyChart so the property charts agree: light
// #2563EB/#B45309/#7C3AED. It marks which market segment a project sits in — a classification,
// never a verdict. No metric is coloured by whether it is "good": a shorter walk, a longer lease
// and a higher price are not virtues in common, and the point of a comparison is that the reader
// weighs them rather than us. Every column carries its project name and segment code, so meaning
// never rests on colour alone.
//
// DIRECTION IS STATED, NEVER IMPLIED. Each row says what its bar measures and which way it runs,
// because half of these have no better end. "Against its district" is the sharpest case: it is a
// position, not a discount or a markup, and a newer building, a higher floor or a shorter walk
// can each explain it.
//
// The default three are the highest-volume projects in the set, picked by the data rather than by
// us, precisely so the starting state is not an editorial choice.
const ASOF = 'August 2026';
const MIN_SALES = 10;
const TOTAL_PROJECTS = 1135;

